  "summary": {
    "en": {
      "caveats": "The study relies on cross-sectional data from a single year (2021), which limits the ability to establish long-term causal trends between ICT adoption and SDG progress.",
      "what": "This study analyzed the impact of 9 household and individual information and communication technology (ICT) variables on 3 socio-economic Sustainable Development Goal (SDG) factors across 235 European regions in 2021. The researchers compared a multilevel mixed linear regression model with a spatial error model (SEM) using block weights, grouping the regions into 9 performance clusters.",
      "why": "It demonstrates that basic ICT activities like social media use have a stronger positive impact in lagging regions (such as Southeastern Europe), whereas advanced digital services like e-banking and digital government interaction are more strongly associated with higher SDG achievements in Northern and Central Europe. This provides a geographically differentiated framework for targeting regional digital policies rather than applying a one-size-fits-all approach."
    }
  },
